{
 "turns": [
  {
   "idx": 0,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Hi, Mr.",
   "start": 0.21,
   "end": 1.07
  },
  {
   "idx": 1,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Whittaker. I'm Megan Kowalczyk,",
   "start": 1.31,
   "end": 4.08
  },
  {
   "idx": 2,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "the nurse practitioner in the Heart Failure Clinic. Can you confirm your full name and date of birth for me?",
   "start": 5.23,
   "end": 13.86
  },
  {
   "idx": 3,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Darnell Whittaker, February 27, 1958",
   "start": 14.58,
   "end": 18.24
  },
  {
   "idx": 4,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Thank you. The nurse said you called on Monday about some swelling.",
   "start": 18.84,
   "end": 24.47
  },
  {
   "idx": 5,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "What's been happening? Yeah, my ankles have been puffing up for about a week. My shoes are tight by the afternoon and the scale says I'm up seven pounds. Okay, what's your usual weight at home? I'm usually right around 182. This morning it was 189.",
   "start": 25.9,
   "end": 43.56
  },
  {
   "idx": 6,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "And how's your breathing?",
   "start": 44.76,
   "end": 45.84
  },
  {
   "idx": 7,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Worse than usual, normally I can walk three blocks to the corner store. Now I'm winded after about one block.",
   "start": 46.66,
   "end": 52.8
  },
  {
   "idx": 8,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "How many pillows are you sleeping on?",
   "start": 53.44,
   "end": 55.74
  },
  {
   "idx": 9,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "I've gone from one pillow to two, feels easier to breathe propped up.",
   "start": 55.74,
   "end": 60.46
  },
  {
   "idx": 10,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Are you waking up at night,",
   "start": 60.76,
   "end": 62.12
  },
  {
   "idx": 11,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "gasping for air? No not that.",
   "start": 62.84,
   "end": 65.51
  },
  {
   "idx": 12,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Any chest pain, or feeling like you might pass out?",
   "start": 66.31,
   "end": 70.42
  },
  {
   "idx": 13,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "No chest pain, no passing out.",
   "start": 71.1,
   "end": 72.94
  },
  {
   "idx": 14,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Anything different with your diet, the last couple of weeks.",
   "start": 73.72,
   "end": 77.77
  },
  {
   "idx": 15,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Well my nephew got married two weekends ago, there was a lot of ham, a lot of salty food, Dude, I wasn't careful, I'll admit it. My wife Lorraine already gave me an earful. That happens.",
   "start": 78.39,
   "end": 89.17
  },
  {
   "idx": 16,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Let's go through your medicines. You're on sacubitril Valsartan. 49 and 51 milligrams twice a day. Carvedilol 12 .5 milligrams twice a day. Spironolactone 25 milligrams once a and Dapagliff lives in 10 milligrams once a day. Still taking all of those.",
   "start": 90.48,
   "end": 116.22
  },
  {
   "idx": 17,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Every one of them, Lorraine fills my pill box on Sundays.",
   "start": 117.18,
   "end": 120.14
  },
  {
   "idx": 18,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "And the furor smite, how much are you taking?",
   "start": 120.72,
   "end": 123.7
  },
  {
   "idx": 19,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "The water pill is 20 milligrams in the morning. Wait, no, it's the 40. They changed it in the summer, it's 40.",
   "start": 124.51,
   "end": 131.49
  },
  {
   "idx": 20,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Yes, 40 milligrams once a day is what I have. Anything over the counter.",
   "start": 132.33,
   "end": 138.91
  },
  {
   "idx": 21,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Um, I've been taking naproxen for my knees. The arthritis acts up. Two of the 220 milligram pills twice a day for about two weeks.",
   "start": 139.04,
   "end": 148.52
  },
  {
   "idx": 22,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Okay, that's important. I'll come back to that. Let me check you over. Your weight here is 189 pounds. Blood pressure is 112 over 70, pulse 78, and oxygen is 95 percent on room air, your neck veins are up to about 10 centimeters. I hear some crackles at the bottom of both lungs. Heart is regular, and I hear a soft extra sound called an S3, and you've got two -plus pitting swelling up to the middle of both shins.",
   "start": 149.28,
   "end": 193.88
  },
  {
   "idx": 23,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "That doesn't sound great.",
   "start": 194.3,
   "end": 195.42
  },
  {
   "idx": 24,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "It tells me you're holding onto extra fluid. Your last echo in March showed an ejection fraction of 30 percent, so your heart pump is weaker than normal and it's sensitive to salt. Your labs from three weeks ago were okay, potassium 4, 6, and creatinine 1 .2.",
   "start": 195.92,
   "end": 221.9
  },
  {
   "idx": 25,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "So what do we do?",
   "start": 222.42,
   "end": 223.44
  },
  {
   "idx": 26,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "So this is a mild flare of your heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. I think the salty food and the naproxen both played a part. Naproxen makes the kidneys hold onto salt and water. So I want you to stop it completely.",
   "start": 223.94,
   "end": 245.34
  },
  {
   "idx": 27,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "What do I take for the knees then?",
   "start": 246.0,
   "end": 247.44
  },
  {
   "idx": 28,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Acetaminophen is safer for your heart. You can take 650 milligrams, up to three times a day. For the fluid, I'm increasing the furosemide to 40 milligrams twice a day. for the next five days. Once your weight is back near 183, go back to 40 milligrams once a day.",
   "start": 248.34,
   "end": 275.85
  },
  {
   "idx": 29,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Okay, 40 twice a day for five days.",
   "start": 276.61,
   "end": 278.67
  },
  {
   "idx": 30,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Right. Keep all your other heart medicines the same. Weigh yourself every morning after you use the bathroom and write it down. Keep salt under 2 ,000 milligrams a day and fluids to about two liters.",
   "start": 279.27,
   "end": 297.54
  },
  {
   "idx": 31,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Lorraine's going to love that she's been on me about the salt.",
   "start": 298.26,
   "end": 301.4
  },
  {
   "idx": 32,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "I'd also like a basic metabolic panel on the 21st of October to make sure your kidneys and potassium are okay with a higher dose. Call us if your weight goes up three pounds in a day or five pounds in a week. If you're short of breath at rest or you get chest pain go to the emergency room.",
   "start": 302.03,
   "end": 330.41
  },
  {
   "idx": 33,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Got it.",
   "start": 331.07,
   "end": 331.45
  },
  {
   "idx": 34,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "And are you using the patient portal?",
   "start": 332.01,
   "end": 334.17
  },
  {
   "idx": 35,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "I can send you the instructions there. Yes, it's Darnell .Wittaker at ExampleyMail .com.",
   "start": 335.67,
   "end": 341.75
  },
  {
   "idx": 36,
   "speaker": "clinician",
   "text": "Perfect. I'll send the plan there, and I want to see you back in one week to check your weight and the swelling.",
   "start": 342.33,
   "end": 350.39
  },
  {
   "idx": 37,
   "speaker": "patient",
   "text": "Sounds good. Thank you, Megan.",
   "start": 351.36,
   "end": 352.58
  }
 ],
